w: Still, still with Thee, _ when pur- ple morn- ing break- eth, When the bird wak- eth, and the sha- dows flee;
w: A- lone with Thee, _ a- mid the mys- tic sha- dows, The so- lemn hush of na- ture new- ly born;
w: As in the daw- _ ning o'er the wave- less o- cean The im- age of the morn- ing star doth rest,
w: When sinks the soul, _ sub- dued by toil, to slum- ber, Its clo- sing eye looks up to Thee in prayer;
w: So shall it be _ at last, in that bright morn- ing, When the soul wak- eth and life’s sha- dows flee;
G2 F3/2 E/ | e3 d | c B A3/2 G/ | c2 F2 | F2 F3/2 G/ | A3 F | C D G3/2 F/ | E4 |]
w: Fai- rer than mor- ning, lo- ve- lier than day- light, Dawns the sweet con- scious- ness, I am with Thee.
w: A- lone with Thee in breath- less a- do- ra- tion, In the calm dew and fresh- ness of the morn.
w: So in the still- ness Thou be- hold- est on- ly Thine im- age in the wa- ters of my breast.
w: Sweet the re- pose be- neath the wings o’er- shad- ing, But sweet- er still to wake and find Thee there.
w: O in that hour, fair- er than day- light dawn- ing, Shall rise the glo- rious thought, I am with Thee.
